rds the message to any  clients that are subscribed to it and I  really like this architecture because  the publisher and the subscribers don't  need to know about each other they don't  have to run at the same time and  operations on both components are not  halted during the publishing receiving  of messages it's super super lightweight  so it's transported over tcp/ip it only  has a 2 by overhead and it's just they  meant to keep it this way they meant to  keep these package headers really small  and every bit in that packet it's just  carefully crafted to remove to reduce  the amount of data transmitted over the  network it means that in comparison to  other protocols like HTTP it doesn't  load the network with transfer of  information that's just necessary to  allow the protocol to run and then  having this small overhead is just  really ideal for IOT because it lowers  the amount of data that might be  transmitted over really constrained  networks another thing I really like is  the MQTT has a really short and readable  spec and it uses simple verbs to  describe actions basically there's five  verbs in MQTT connect/disconnect  publish/subscribe and ping so I was  really jazzed up about MQTT and I really  couldn't wait to switch out my socket IO  clients and server and use mqtt instead  so I started with the web app and for  implementing this in the browser there's  n QT tjs library and you can use it in  the browser and it's written in  JavaScript so you can use it a note in  the browser and so I think the code is  pretty  easy to follow and that's one reason I  really liked implementing this because  there's only really a few lines so I  just require the library and then I  create a client that connects to the URL  wherever in the Internet I have my  broker Connect created and then in this  example if I have like a rainbow but and  that people can click on the change my  clothes so I just create a function so  whenever that button is clicked it sends  a message to the broker with the topic  lights and that's what I would have my  clients my outfit clients to subscribe  to and and then it can just send the  payload string of rainbow next I set up  the clients on the Adreno  so this is even if you're not familiar  kind of with adreno C++ code is pretty  much the same as the other client so  they just include the library I tell  it's where the broker is located which  URL on the web where to find it and it  subscribes to the topic lights and then  whenever messages receive just this on  message message method is called and  then it can just interpret the message  sent and then figure out what program  Anisa to switch to so I had my clients  set up it was all going like pretty  smoothly and the next was I needed to  set the broker so everything could talk  to each other and just want to show this  slide again so like I mentioned before I  deployed everything just on one Express  server on Heroku and when I went to go  set up my broker even though I really  loved Heroku because it has a really  great CL CLI that's easy to use I found  out you don't have port access you can't  specify specific ports and for this MQTT  protocol the port that specified that  you need to use for communication is one  eight eight three so that was a slight  hiccup because I couldn't actually build  my own MQTT broker yeah so I couldn't I  didn't have access support I couldn't I  couldn't set this up but I was also not  quite ready to leave her okay  kind of just wanted to see if all this  stuff was going to actually work  together while before I actually moved  to a different service so I went looking  for an external MQTT broker service and  it turns out there's a bunch of them the  one I ended up choosing is called  shifter I oh it's just a IOT prototyping  platform I chose it cuz it's free and  it's pretty easy sub you just give your  email and you get a key and a token and  compared to other mqtt broker services  it doesn't seem to limit the number of  messages they can receive per minute  some of them do and you have to actually  pay if you receive more than a few  messages a minute so I tried it out and  it worked and it works like really  really well compared to my first attempt  but I was happy but I wasn't ecstatic  because here it was I was replying  relying on this small external broker  service and that really just wasn't  optimal for me because there's just a  lot of unknowns there's not I didn't  know that much about this company and I  wanted to make something really reliable  and I really felt to make it really  reliable I needed to have control over  it so as you can probably guess the next  thing I did was I decided to create my  own broker and I had to do that I had to  move from Heroku to digitalocean  which gave me a little bit of anxiety  just because I've not that's comfortable  or skilled yet in doing like dev Ops II  kind of stuff but it worked out so it's  a you shouldn't be scared of that if  you're thinking about it's moving to a  service like that so to build this I  used this broker library called ATS and  what I really liked about this library  is just besides being you know using  streams and just it's a really new  modern library is that the broker is an  embeddable and my it would be embeddable  my current express server that I was  using to serve my website so I would  really have minimal configuration change  so I was pretty happy I would have to  build a second server for just handling  these messages  so to implement them for the mqtt broker  i just include the library and create  the MQTT server an HTTP server because  like I said this was also serving my web  app I just specified the ports that each  of those need to listen to so for mqtt  is someone 83 and then my app was just  on 8080 and then at the bottom you can  see that and I think this is really cool  as well is I can add on so that the HTTP  HTTP server that's serving my website it  can have MQTT over WebSockets  capabilities so those messages coming  from my web app they're just served  using WebSockets from the HTTP server  and I was pretty excited because this  was working really good and I had  figured out a lot of things and I  conquered DevOps fears so I was pretty  excited the last thing I did because I  really wanted to make this rock-solid  which I hope in a few minutes that I  showed that it's rock-solid is that I  upgraded the microcontroller so I  decided to change to the Adafruit  feather in 0y5 and there's a couple  reasons for that so it has low power  management so I wanted to stretch as  much out of my battery life as I could  and the main reason was that it has a  separate Wi-Fi module so what that means  it doesn't have to yield all the time to  the Wi-Fi course since it's a separate  chip so you get full rein of the  processor and timing and it just means  you just have really high speed reliable  Wi-Fi that doesn't crash there is a  downside and the downside is that is  twice the cost of the other  microcontroller I was using so the  feather hisses around 18 euros and this  cost around 35 but that was a downside  that wasn't it was worth the extra cost  to get the reliability so it's demo time  so this is probably the biggest audience  that I've had that can interact with  this so I invite you to please go to  flashy lights dot NL and you can play  around with my web app and change my  perfect  you can change what it looks like I'll  just preference this with this is meant  to be like a one-on-one experience so it  might get a little glitchy with so many  people sending to it at the same time  but what the heck like let's go for it  so while you're playing around at flashy  law I saw you know I just want to leave  you kind of with my closing thoughts  that you might not be aware but I am a  super introvert and this was a really  weird experiment to do as an introvert I  am NOT one that you know attracting  attention and actively seeking people to  out to interact with me is completely  out of my comfort zone sorry but it was  also an experiment that really pushed me  to deal with my own reactions to those  interactions and I just really wanted to  see if I could bring like a momentary  joy and whimsy to strangers by giving  them free rein over my appearance and  really outside of our tech bubble this  is not an application this is an  application like this is not one that  most people have seen and experienced so  is really I also found that I was kind  of breaking stereotypes about tech with  strangers like they were kind of taken  aback when they realized that I had  built this and you could do these kind  of weird experiments with tech and kind  of question what you know what you can  build and who is capable building those  things in tech and I said thank you for  listening  I am I also build other fun things with  LEDs so if you're interested in seeing  those I'm terrible at blogging at things  but I post pictures on Twitter and later  tonight I made another piece that's not  interactive but it's still really really  cool that I want to wear tonight at the  party so if you see me say hi don't be  shy thank you  [Applause]
 

